From 69d83e2cbf6b0f6dd7c516a7262fba6aa32d6e27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ryevdokimov <105675984+ryevdokimov@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 23:19:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] OIP fork: native conveyor listener, batched body
 snapshot, coupled planar friction

Three additions carried on top of upstream tag 1.1.0 (Jolt C++ 5.6.0), used by
oip-web's sim worker and vendored into that repo as a prebuilt tarball.

- ConveyorContactListener (JoltJS.h/.idl): fully-native contact listener that
  reads per-facet surface velocities from a fixed-capacity table and applies the
  two ghost gates, replacing a per-contact JS listener whose WASM->JS crossings
  dominated step cost (and which the multithreaded build cannot run at all,
  since contact callbacks fire on pthread job threads with isolated JS realms).
- BodySnapshot (JoltJS.h/.idl): one call fills a flat float buffer with
  pos+quat per requested body, replacing ~9 getter crossings per body per frame.
- patches/0001-coupled-planar-friction.patch: adds ContactSettings::
  mFrictionCoupling to Jolt itself, shrinking the torsional friction limit by
  the fraction of the tangential limit consumed. Without it, conveyed cargo that
  catches a corner dead-stops instead of pivoting off it. Defaults to 0, which
  is bit-identical to upstream. Applied via PATCH_COMMAND in CMakeLists.txt.
- CMakeLists.txt: applies the patch set, sizes the pthread pool at runtime, and
  restores the parens that closure's --dynamic_import_alias round-trip drops
  from emscripten's node pthread self-detection (which otherwise hangs MT init).

Rationale, measurements and known limitations for the Jolt-core patch are in
patches/README.md; read it before bumping GIT_TAG, since a version bump needs
the patch re-checked against the new solver rather than merely re-applied.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
