@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+# OIP patches against upstream Jolt
+
+Applied by `PATCH_COMMAND` in `FetchContent_Declare(JoltPhysics ...)` (CMakeLists.txt), once
+when the dependency is populated. To re-apply after editing a patch, wipe `Build/` so
+FetchContent re-clones, or apply by hand in each `Build/*/_deps/joltphysics-src`.
+
+These are patches to **Jolt itself**, distinct from the fork's additions in `JoltJS.h` /
+`JoltJS.idl` (`ConveyorContactListener`, `BodySnapshot`), which are edits to this repo.
+
+Read this before bumping `GIT_TAG` — each patch is a semantic change to upstream code, so a
+version bump needs the patch re-checked against the new source, not just re-applied.
+
+## 0001-coupled-planar-friction.patch
+
+Pinned against Jolt `v5.6.0` (e77f1755).
+
+**What upstream does.** Jolt solves contact friction at the manifold level with three planar
+DOFs: two linear tangents (`mFrictionConstraint1/2`) plus a torsional constraint about the
+normal (`mAngularFrictionConstraint`), all applied at the averaged contact point. Both budgets
+derive from the same normal impulse and combined friction:
+
+```
+max_linear_lambda  = friction * Sum(lambda_n)
+max_angular_lambda = friction * Sum(|r| * lambda_n)
+```
+
+The linear pair is clamped **jointly** (a proper friction cone). The torsional constraint is
+clamped **independently**, with no reference to how much of the linear budget was consumed.
+
+**Why that's wrong.** Tangential and torsional friction physically draw on the same Coulomb
+budget. A body already sliding has spent its traction dragging forward and should offer almost
+no resistance to spinning. Solved with independent limits the two are additive, so a sliding
+body resists yaw exactly as hard as a stationary one.
+
+Measured on stock v5.6.0, a 10 kg box on a belt: yaw resistance breaks at the same torque
+(~21 N.m = mu*N*r) whether the belt is slipping under a stalled box or stopped. Lateral
+translation, by contrast, correctly loses its budget when the drive saturates. The consequence
+in an industrial sim: conveyed cargo that catches a corner on an obstruction **dead-stops
+instead of pivoting off it**, and it can never pivot, for any box geometry - the catch's couple
+is bounded by the drive force times half the box width, always less than the torsional limit's
+lever arm of the footprint's corner radius.
+
+**What the patch does.** Adds `ContactSettings::mFrictionCoupling` (default `0.0f`) and, when
+it is > 0, shrinks the torsional limit by the fraction of the tangential limit consumed:
+
+```
+angular_limit *= Sqrt(max(1 - mFrictionCoupling * consumed, 0))
+```
+
+which is the projection onto an ellipsoidal limit surface. At `0.0` the arithmetic is
+bit-identical to upstream, so every contact that does not opt in is unaffected.
+
+**Known limitation.** The coupling is one-directional: linear saturation reduces torsional
+capacity, but not the reverse (a spinning body keeps full linear grip). That covers the
+conveyor case and keeps the default path exact. A symmetric version would solve all three
+lambdas and project them together, which needs a get/apply split on
+`AngularFrictionConstraintPart` mirroring the one `ContactConstraintPart` already has.
+
+**Second-order effect, not addressed here.** `Sum(|r| * lambda_n)` puts every contact point at
+the footprint's corner radius (0.361 m for a 0.6 x 0.4 m box) where a real distributed pressure
+patch integrates to the area-mean (~0.19 m), so torsional friction runs ~1.9x high regardless of
+coupling. If pivots still feel sticky with coupling at 1.0, that weighting is the next knob.
+
+**Consumers.** `ConveyorContactListener::SetFrictionCoupling()` (JoltJS.h) sets it on driven
+deck contacts only - the ghost gates and undriven registered bodies return before that point.
+`ContactSettings.mFrictionCoupling` is also exposed to `ContactListenerJS` via JoltJS.idl.
diff --git a/patches/apply.cmake b/patches/apply.cmake
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..91d8a79
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/apply.cmake
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# Idempotently apply the OIP patches to the fetched Jolt tree (see patches/README.md).
+#
+# FetchContent re-runs PATCH_COMMAND every time the populate sub-build re-runs -- e.g. after a
+# failed configure -- so a bare `git apply` succeeds once and then fails with "patch does not
+# apply" on every retry. Reverse-check first and skip anything already applied.
+#
+# Runs with the working directory set to the fetched source tree, so git operates on it directly.
+
+foreach(patch IN LISTS PATCHES)
+	execute_process(
+		COMMAND git apply --whitespace=nowarn --reverse --check ${patch}
+		RESULT_VARIABLE already_applied
+		ERROR_QUIET OUTPUT_QUIET)
+	if(already_applied EQUAL 0)
+		message(STATUS "OIP Jolt patch already applied, skipping: ${patch}")
+	else()
+		execute_process(
+			COMMAND git apply --whitespace=nowarn ${patch}
+			RESULT_VARIABLE apply_result)
+		if(NOT apply_result EQUAL 0)
+			message(FATAL_ERROR "Failed to apply OIP Jolt patch: ${patch}")
+		endif()
+		message(STATUS "OIP Jolt patch applied: ${patch}")
+	endif()
+endforeach()
